From what I can tell based on a quick dip into the source, there's a
function (bound_digging() in mkmaze.c) which gets called during level
creation and essentially marks as undiggable every square outside the
mimimum rectangle needed to contain every room and other dungeon
feature created so far.

I'm not sure what the point of the function is.  In fact, it seems to
leak some information to the player that should presumably be hidden:
if you can dig outside the bounds of what you've discovered so far,
you know there must be something else to be found in that direction.

Also, given the existence of this function, I'm not sure why the
message given when trying to dig outside the maximum possible extent
of the level (which can happen if the dungeon, and thus the diggable
area, extend all the way to the edge) is different from the message
given when you hit this undiggable boundary ("Clash!" vs. "This wall
is too hard to dig into.").  It could be that this is a bug in the
level creation code, and that the undiggable boundary is actually
supposed to surround every level on all sides.

